# The annotations of RAM bank 6 at $C000: the source of src/bank6/*.asm (../../kit/mkgame.py --part src/bank6/bank6.json).
# Format: ../../../../docs/ann-format.md. Witnesses: the recordings of work/stage1/rec (WALKTHROUGH 6.0), page 6.

@module pointer ; The mouse pointer: its shapes, the saved background, the save, restore, shift and draw
@about The menus show a pointer of 8 lines by 16 pixels. The interrupt (InterruptHandler $8383) moves it: RestorePointerBackground $D251 puts back the screen bytes saved in PointerBackground $C020, SavePointerBackground $D0CA saves the bytes at the new place, DrawPointer $D27C shifts the shape (ShiftPointer $D282, the shape's address in its operand $D283) to the pixel and puts it on with its mask (PutPointer $D2F1). Over a 3D window (the title's and the stores' model) the pointer goes into the back buffer instead (CopyViewUnderPointer $D142, DrawPointerInView $D1BE), so the blit of the view carries it.
@about The position comes from PointerPos $7DC0 through CopyPointer $897E ($7C51 X, $7C53 Y); the limits of the pointer are set from PointerLimits $D32B.

$C000 b PointerShapes 2 ; The two pointer shapes: 8 lines of a mask byte and a pixel byte each
> Line n is two bytes: the mask (`AND` with the screen, shifted in with ones) and the pixels (`OR`). ShiftPointer `$D282` reads the shape its operand `$D283` points at. The arrow at `$C000` is set by the catalogue, the briefing and the stores; the boxed shape at `$C010` by ShowWaitPointer `$CBF4` when a screen is left, so it shows while the next one is drawn.
$C000 ArrowPointer ; the arrow
$C010 WaitPointer ; the boxed shape shown between screens

$C020 b PointerBackground 2 ; The 8 x 2 screen bytes under the pointer
> Written by SavePointerBackground `$D0CA` (from the screen) and CopyViewUnderPointer `$D142` (from the back buffer), put back by RestorePointerBackground `$D251`.

@module catalogue ; The mission catalogue screen: the list of the ten missions from the catalogue, the selection, the description
@about MissionCatalogueScreen $C790 is screen node $7504 of the screen list (ScreenList $74E6). On the first visit the catalogue is not yet in memory (IsCatalogueLoaded $C870 compares its head with "MISSION"): the screen asks for the mission tape and loads it (LoadDirectory $715D). Then BuildMissionNames $C030 makes ten printable lines from the catalogue's names, PrintMissionList $CFA9 prints them, a tick (TickGraphic $C978) marks every completed mission (MissionsDone $6799), and the pointer's zones (CatalogueZones $C851) load a mission, pick a line, go to the briefing or the stores.

$C030 c BuildMissionNames ; Copy the catalogue's head, then build the ten mission name lines at $93D9 with their pointers at $93B9
$C042 NameLoop
$C04E NameCopy
$C057 NamePad
$C059 NamePadLoop
> CopyCatalogueHead `$70C6` puts the catalogue's first 1 K into the back buffer at `$8AF1`. For each of the ten 27-byte names there: code 13 with the attribute `$46`, the name up to its 0 byte, spaces to 27 characters, code 11 and the end `$FF`; the line's address goes to the next word of `$93B9`.
> Output: the strings in `$93D9-$950E` and the table `$93B9-$93CC` (in the back buffer, bank 2). Witness: L1 32 (c1, death, m10, m2erbe, m2ocean, menu, missions, play, play2, search).

$C06F c WaitForClick ; Wait until the interrupt's fire test sets ClickFlag $6D43, then clear it
$C072 WaitClickLoop
> Known only from the code: nothing calls it.

@module statusview ; The console's BRIEF page box and the grid position of the status view
@about The status view (StatusLoop $6BC7) and its BRIEF page (BriefPage $6C44) are drawn by the resident code in bank 2; bank 6 holds the parts they borrow: the boxes behind the briefing text of the BRIEF page, the GRID Y / GRID X numbers (PrintStatusGrid $C0F7), the map of the status view (module map) and the message of the status views (module panel).

$C079 c BriefPageBox ; The attribute box $C0B1 and 8 cleared pixel lines behind the BRIEF page's text
> Called once by BriefPageText `$70B6`. AttrBox `$6CFB` with `$C0B1`, then ScreenRowFill `$6D22` of 8 lines of 18 bytes from line `$88`, column 7 (through BriefPageFill). Witness: L1 1 (play).

$C089 b BriefPageBoxRecord 5 ; An attribute box record (width, height, column, attribute, row) for the unused variant $C08E
> Read only by BriefPageBoxSmall.

$C08E c BriefPageBoxSmall ; Variant: the box $C089, then the fill of BriefPageBoxLarge
> Known only from the code: nothing calls it.

$C096 c BriefPageBoxLarge ; Variant: the box $C0AC and 40 cleared lines at column 10
> Known only from the code: nothing calls it; its tail BriefPageFill is the one BriefPageBox jumps to.
$C09C BriefPageLarge
$C0A4 BriefPageFill ; A' = $FF, A lines, BC the column: ScreenRowFill of 18 bytes from line $88

$C0AC b BriefPageBoxes 5 ; Four attribute box records of the briefing texts
> `$C0AC` read by BriefPageBoxLarge, `$C0B1` by BriefPageBox, `$C0B6` by BriefingTextOnce (`$70A8`), `$C0BB` by BriefPageText (`$70C0`); none of the four is read inside bank 6.
$C0B1 BriefPageBoxB
$C0B6 BriefPageBoxC
$C0BB BriefPageBoxD

$C0C0 c FormatGridPosition ; Format the map position: the two numbers in the operands $C0C7 and $C0E2 into GridText
> The operands are written by DrawStatusMap `$DB1C` from the view's position (`$9A6C` and `$9A8E`). Each goes through NumberToDigits `$C126` (leading zeros kept, C = `0`) into PaddedDigits `$D9AB`; digits 2-3 and 4-5 are copied around the colon of GridText. Witness: L1 40 (m2erbe, missions, play).
$C0C6 GridYOp ; LD HL,n: n ($C0C7) the Y position
$C0E1 GridXOp ; LD HL,n: n ($C0E2) the X position

$C0F7 c PrintStatusGrid ; Print the grid position of the status view
> Called every frame by StatusLoop `$6BC7`: FormatGridPosition, then PrintString `$6FB3` with GridText. Witness: L1 40 (m2erbe, missions, play).

$C101 b GridText 16 ; Text: the two positions "00:00" at line $50 and line $70, column 1
> Control code 2 (a pixel line and a column), code 13 (attribute `$47`); the digits at `$C106` and `$C10E` are written by FormatGridPosition.
@res texts/grid-text.bin

@module stores ; The stores screen: the weapons catalogue, the four launcher slots, the payload, the turning model
@about StoresScreen $CA8B is screen node $7514 (zones StoresZones $CBC4). It unpacks the bank 7 picture PicStores ($C713 of bank 7), shows one of the 13 weapons of WeaponRecords $C373 at a time (its icon, its description and its 3D model, which turns in a 3D window drawn into the back buffer and blitted by BlitStoresView $D0A0), and lets the player fit it into one of four slots ($8368, four 6-byte records) or take one out. The total weight (the operand $C11B) may not pass 1000 kg, and a weapon may be fitted only as many times as its record allows.
@about When the player leaves the screen, StoresToGauges $CC1B turns the slots into the panel's gauges and the screen list goes on to StartMission $694B (link 0 of the node).

$C114 c PrintPayloadWeight ; Print the payload's weight in kg on the payload line
> NumberToDigits `$C126` with C = `:` (the space of SmallFont `$7C59`) for the leading zeros into `$D9AB`, then PrintString `$6FB3` of PayloadText `$D9A8`. Witness: L1 24 (c1, death, m2erbe, missions, play, play2).
$C11A PayloadWeightOp ; LD HL,n: n ($C11B) the payload's total weight in kg

@module numbers ; Numbers to decimal digits
@about Two converters: NumberToDigits $C126 for 16-bit numbers (five digits: the stores' weight and the grid position) and LongToDigits $D353 for the 32-bit score (ten digits).

$C126 c NumberToDigits ; Write HL as five ASCII digits at HL', then replace the leading zeros at $D9AB with C'
$C148 BlankZeroLoop
$C154 BlankZeroDone
> Input: HL the number, HL' the destination, C' the character for a leading zero. DigitOf `$C155` for 10000, 1000, 100 and 10, then the units. Then the five digits of PaddedDigits `$D9AB` from the left: each `0` becomes C' until the first other digit; a number 0 keeps its last `0`. Witness: L1 104 (c1, death, m2erbe, missions, play, play2).

$C155 c DigitOf ; One decimal digit: A = how many times DE goes into HL, stored at HL' as ASCII
$C157 DigitCount
> Input: HL, DE. Output: HL the remainder, HL' advanced. Witness: L1 416 (c1, death, m2erbe, missions, play, play2).

@module stores

$C165 c SlotZoneB ; Zone of the slot at the top right: C = $24 (slot record $836E)
> Called through PointerZones `$6D42` (StoresZones). Witness: L1 3 (play, play2).

$C169 c SlotZoneA ; Zone of the slot at the top left: C = $1E (slot record $8368)
> Witness: L1 15 (c1, death, m2erbe, missions, play, play2).

$C16D c SlotZoneC ; Zone of the slot at the bottom left: C = $2A (slot record $8374)
> Witness: L1 3 (play, play2).

$C171 c SlotZoneD ; Zone of the slot at the bottom right: C = $30 (slot record $837A)
> Falls into FitOrRemoveWeapon. Witness: L1 3 (play, play2).

$C173 c FitOrRemoveWeapon ; Fit the shown weapon into slot $834A+C, or take out the weapon that is in it
> Three POKEs of the release's .pok patch it (bank 6, the stores screen): Ammo Number Is Not Exceeded 1 at `$C1AE` (`JP Z,$C235` becomes `LD BC,nn`), Infinite Payload Weight 0 at `$C1B9` (the `ADD HL,BC` of the weight becomes NOP), No Weight Limit 58 at `$C1C6` (`JP NC,$C23A` becomes `LD A,(nn)`).
$C19A CountSameType
> Input: C the slot's offset from `$834A`. A slot record is the weapon's type, its weight (word), the icon's column and line, its ammunition. An empty slot: the weapon's count in the slots is compared with its maximum (record +2; equal: MaxNumberText `$D990`), the new total with 1001 kg (PayloadExceededText `$D963`); when both pass, the slot gets the type, weight and ammunition, DrawSlotIcon `$C263` draws the icon, and PrintPayloadWeight the new total. A full slot: RemoveWeapon clears it, subtracts its weight and blanks the icon (ClearSlotIcon `$C25C`).
> Around every change the interrupt's pointer job is off (`$83BF` = 0) and the pointer is put back (RestorePointerBackground `$D251`, SavePointerBackground `$D0CA`). An error message stays for 60 frames (StoresMessageTimer `$C2C0`). Witness: L1 24 (c1, death, m2erbe, missions, play, play2).
$C19B SlotTypeOp ; CP n: n ($C19C) the shown weapon's type
$C1A0 CountNextSlot
$C1D4 SlotWeightOp ; LD BC,n: n ($C1D5) the shown weapon's weight
$C1E6 SlotAmmoOp ; LD A,n: n ($C1E7) the shown weapon's ammunition
$C1FF RemoveWeapon
$C235 MaxNumberExceeded
$C23A PayloadExceeded
$C23D ShowStoresMessage

$C257 b StoresMessageBox 5 ; The attribute box of the error message line
> Read by FitOrRemoveWeapon at `$C23E`.

$C25C c ClearSlotIcon ; Draw a blank icon (the back buffer's $92C1) into the slot at column C', line A
> Falls into DrawSlotIcon with HL' = `$92C1`.

$C263 c DrawSlotIcon ; Draw the 3 x 24 icon at HL' into a slot: column C', pixel line A
$C273 SlotIconTop
$C28D SlotIconBottom
> The line address table `$6500`. The first 8 lines are copied two bytes wide one column to the right, the next 16 three bytes wide. Witness: L1 24 (c1, death, m2erbe, missions, play, play2).

$C2A1 c ResetStores ; A RET: the slot clearing that follows is switched off
> Called by StoresScreen `$CACE`. The first byte is `RET`, so ResetStoresBody never runs; ClearSlots `$CC01` does the work instead. Witness: L1 15 (c1, death, m2erbe, missions, play, play2).

$C2A2 c ResetStoresBody ; Clear the 9 records at $834A, the payload weight and the hook $A9B9
$C2AB ResetStoresLoop
> Known only from the code: behind the `RET` of ResetStores. Clears the first three bytes of nine 6-byte records from `$834A`, the weight `$C11B` = 0, `$A9B9` = `$6BB0` (NoAction).

$C2C0 c StoresMessageTimer ; Count down the error message; at 0 clear the message line and print the payload again
> Called every frame by StoresScreen. The counter is the operand `$C2C1` of the first instruction (60 after a message). At 0: ScreenRowFill `$6D22`, the box PayloadLineBox, PayloadLineText `$D936`, PrintPayloadWeight, the pointer put back. Witness: L1 2525 (c1, death, m2erbe, missions, play, play2).
$C2CB ClearStoresMessage

$C2F9 b PayloadLineBox 5 ; The attribute box of the payload lines
> Read by StoresMessageTimer `$C2DE`.

$C2FE c ShowWeapon ; Show the weapon CurrentWeapon points at: its icon, its description, its model
> ClearWeaponPanel `$C363`, CopyGraphic `$6D7F` of the 3 x 24 icon (record +5) at line `$30`, column 2, PrintString of the description (record +7), and the model (record +9) into StoresModelOp, the operand `$CB3B` of the stores' 3D loop. Witness: L1 54 (c1, death, m2erbe, missions, play, play2).

$C32E c NextWeapon ; Zone: the next record of WeaponRecords (the first again after the end mark), then ShowWeapon
$C342 NextWeaponSet
> Witness: L1 39 (play, play2).

$C347 c PreviousWeapon ; Zone: the record before (the last one before the start mark), then ShowWeapon
$C358 PreviousWeaponStep
> Known only from the code: in StoresZones (`$CBD0`), but no recording clicks it.

$C363 c ClearWeaponPanel ; Clear 48 pixel lines of 11 bytes at column 3 from line $30 (the weapon's icon and text)
> ScreenRowFill `$6D22` with 0. Witness: L1 54 (c1, death, m2erbe, missions, play, play2).

$C371 w CurrentWeapon 1 ; The record of the shown weapon (WeaponRecords + 1 + 11n)
> Set by StoresScreen `$CAE3`, moved by NextWeapon and PreviousWeapon, read by ShowWeapon and FitOrRemoveWeapon.

$C373 b WeaponRecords 11 ; $FF, the 13 weapon records of 11 bytes, $FF
> A record: +0 the type (1-13; IconTable `$75CA` entry type - 1), +1 the ammunition, +2 the most the four slots may hold, +3 the weight in kg (word), +5 the 3 x 24 icon, +7 the description text, +9 the model script (bank 3 `$CExx`, or bank 2). The `$FF` before and after stop NextWeapon and PreviousWeapon.
> The Sleeper mine's record says 300 kg, its text 290 KG.
$C373 ; the start mark
$C374 WeaponTurret ; type 4 Turret: ammo 80, max 1, 400 kg, icon $F444
$C37F WeaponBansheeRRSS ; type 2: ammo 8, max 2, 300 kg
$C38A WeaponBansheeIRSS ; type 6: ammo 6, max 2, 300 kg
$C395 WeaponPhoenixRRSA ; type 1: ammo 8, max 2, 250 kg
$C3A0 WeaponPhoenixIRSA ; type 5: ammo 6, max 2, 250 kg
$C3AB WeaponK40Mortar ; type 3: ammo 20, max 2, 250 kg
$C3B6 WeaponSleeperMine ; type 9: ammo 5, max 2, 300 kg
$C3C1 WeaponDragonfly ; type 7: ammo 3, max 2, 500 kg
$C3CC WeaponK90Cluster ; type 8: ammo 20, max 4, 250 kg
$C3D7 WeaponSpectre ; type 13: ammo 12, max 2, 100 kg
$C3E2 WeaponPhantasm ; type 12: ammo 8, max 2, 100 kg
$C3ED WeaponSkeetBomb ; type 11: ammo 12, max 2, 200 kg
$C3F8 WeaponSlamLaser ; type 10: ammo 40, max 1, 250 kg
$C403 ; the end mark

$C404 b TitleBox 5 ; The attribute box of the title menu's 3D window
> Read by TitleMenu `$C4A7`.

@module title ; The title menu: the title picture, the turning tank, the three choices
@about TitleMenu $C409 is the first screen (ScreenList node $74E8, zones TitleZones $C4E9); Entry $68F4 jumps here. It unpacks PicTitle ($F4E1 of bank 7), adds three graphics, and runs a 3D window in which the tank turns (Bank4Start $72C6, the model run by Bank3Render $72BE) until a zone is clicked: the catalogue, the options or the password.

$C409 c TitleMenu ; The title menu: picture, graphics, pointer, then the turning model until a zone is clicked
$C46C TitleAttrLoop
> Sound off (Bank0SoundOff `$72DE`), the shadow screen, the view parameters (`$9A6C`, `$9A8E`, `$9AB3`, `$9ABB`), DrawBank7Picture `$74AA` with `$F4E1`, CopyGraphic of TitleGraphicA, TitleGraphicB and TitleGraphicC, the attributes `$5920-$5A1F` to `$47`, the title's pointer limits, the pointer centred, the wide blit (`$83E6` = `$8A41`), SetTitleWindow `$D10E`, Bank4Start `$72C6`, TitleBox.
> TitleLoop: StartViewPass `$9882`, the model through Bank3Render `$72BE` (IX = `$CA8B` of bank 3), RequestViewBlit `$988D`, the turn (`$68E7` += 6), PointerZones `$6D42`, until the view flag `$6A6D` is set; then ShowWaitPointer, WaitFrameDone, Bank4Call and NextView `$6A91`. Witness: L1 19 (c1, m2erbe, menu, missions, play, play2).
$C4AD TitleLoop

$C4DC b StatusScreenZones 6 ; Zone table of the mission status screen: x1, x2, y1, y2, routine
> Read by PointerZones `$6D42` on MissionStatusScreen (node `$756A`): ScreenZero `$705A` (ShowPassword) and ScreenTwo `$705E` (back to the catalogue). A 0 ends it.

$C4E9 b TitleZones 6 ; Zone table of the title menu
> ScreenZero (the catalogue), `$67EE` (ApiVector16, ScreenFour: the options) and ScreenTwo (the password entry).

@module password ; The level entry: the password screen, the line editor, the coding of the progress
@about The progress (the completion flags MissionsDone $6799 and the 32-bit Score $CC79) is packed into ten characters: EncodePassword $CE2E takes 4 bits of the score for each of eight values with a mission's completion flag in bit 4, the last two hold the flags of missions 9-10 and a checksum of the score's bytes; PasswordToChars $CF1C maps the 5-bit values through PasswordAlphabet $CF5F. Entering a password (PasswordScreen $C578) runs the way back: CharsToPassword $CF3E, DecodePassword $CE8B (Z when the checksum agrees), then the flags and the score are taken over.

$C4FC c ShowPassword ; Show the password of the progress so far: "THIS IS YOUR ENTRY STRING." and the ten characters
$C517 ShowPasswordKey
> Screen node `$74FE` (from the mission status screen). ClearMenuScreen, EncodePassword, PasswordToChars, PrintString of EntryStringText and PasswordText `$CEF6`, a delay (Delay `$CD63`), a key, ScreenZero and NextView. Witness: L1 1 (menu).

$C527 c ClearMenuScreen ; Clear the shadow screen's pixels and fill its attributes from the box $C74A
> PageShadowScreen `$7268`, ScreenRowFill of 192 lines of 32 bytes with 0, AttrBox `$C74A`. Witness: L1 6 (c1, menu).

$C53E b PasswordMessages 16 ; Texts: "INVALID PASSWORD", then two blank lines that clear it and the entry
> Printed by PasswordScreen at `$C5A5`, `$C5AE` and `$C5B4`.
$C554 InvalidBlank
$C56A EntryBlank
@res texts/password-messages.bin

$C578 c PasswordScreen ; The level entry screen: read ten characters, check them, take over the flags and the score
> Node `$74F8` (from the title). ClearMenuScreen, PasswordPrompt, the buffer filled with spaces, ReadPassword `$C620`; when the view flag is set (a click elsewhere), back. Otherwise CharsToPassword, DecodePassword: a bad checksum prints "INVALID PASSWORD" and asks again; a good one copies the score to Score `$CC79`, the ten flags to MissionsDone `$6799`, counts them into MissionsCompleted `$CC93`, ScreenZero and NextView. Witness: L1 1 (menu).
$C584 PasswordAgain
$C589 PasswordClearLoop
$C59D PasswordCheck
$C5BC PasswordAccepted
$C5DC CountFlagsLoop
$C5E2 CountFlagsNext

$C5EE b PasswordPrompt 16 ; Text: "Type in your level entry:-"
> Printed by PasswordScreen `$C57B`.
@res texts/password-prompt.bin

$C60E b PasswordBuffer 10 ; The ten characters typed (EditLine's line)
> Filled with spaces by PasswordScreen, written by EditLine, read by CharsToPassword. The image holds leftover code bytes here.

$C618 b PasswordLeftover 8 ; The buffer's end marks, then leftover bytes "...DAMAG": never read
> EditLine writes its end marks to `$C618-$C619`; no routine reads `$C61A-$C61F`.

$C620 c ReadPassword ; A short delay, the key state cleared, then EditLine at the screen place of the entry
$C623 ReadPasswordDelay
> EditLine `$C644` with HL = PasswordBuffer, DE = `$488A` (the pixel address), BC = `$598A` (the attribute address). Witness: L1 1 (menu).

$C644 c EditLine ; Edit a line of up to 10 characters: keys add, DELETE takes back, ENTER ends when it is full
> Input: HL the buffer (set to `$C60E` here), DE the screen address, BC the cursor's attribute address. The state is in operands: the cursor's attribute address CursorAttrPtr, the end of the line (EditEndOp), the length (EditLengthOp), the maximum (EditMaxOp). Each key from WaitKey `$C6EC`: codes 0-1 ignored, 2 leaves through ScreenZero `$705A`, 3 (ENTER) ends when the length is the maximum, 4 (DELETE) takes one back, the others are added up to 14. PrintRawString `$C6FC` redraws the line after every key. Witness: L1 1 (menu).
$C65C EditLineStart
$C673 EditRedraw
$C67C EditNextKey
$C69F EditEndOp ; LD HL,n: n ($C6A0) the end of the typed line
$C6AE EditAddChar
$C6B3 EditMaxOp ; CP n: n ($C6B4) the length that ends the line
$C6D2 EditEnter
$C6E3 EditLengthOp ; LD A,n: n ($C6E4) the length

$C655 c EditLineAt ; Variant entry: A = the maximum, HL = the buffer, then EditLineStart
> Known only from the code: nothing calls it.

$C6AC c EditAddSpace ; Unused: A = space, then the add path EditAddChar
> Known only from the code: no jump reaches it.

$C6EC c WaitKey ; Wait for a new key (bit 0 of KeyState $8978), clear the bit, A = the key code ($8979)
> Witness: L1 286697 (menu).

$C6FA w CursorAttrPtr 1 ; EditLine's cursor attribute address
> Written and read by EditLine.

$C6FC c PrintRawString ; Draw the 0-terminated string at HL straight onto the screen at DE with the 8x8 font
$C706 RawSpaceGlyph
$C70B RawGlyph
$C719 RawGlyphCopy
$C71C RawGlyphLine
> Characters `$20-$7F` take the glyph Font8x8 + 8 (c - `$20`), the others the space; 8 lines down the screen third, one column a character. No attributes, no control codes. Witness: L1 77 (menu).

$C727 w EditLineBuffer 1 ; EditLine's buffer address
> Written by EditLine `$C64E`, read at `$C673`.

$C729 b PasswordZones 1 ; Zone table of the password screen: empty
> PointerZones finds the end at once.

$C72A b EntryStringText 16 ; Text: "THIS IS YOUR ENTRY STRING."
> Printed by ShowPassword.
