@module password

$CE2E c EncodePassword ; Pack MissionsDone and Score into the ten 5-bit values at $CEFB
$CE3E EncodeLoop
$CE5E ChecksumLoop
> Eight values: 4 bits of the score (ScoreBits `$CF06`) and in bit 4 the flag of missions 1-8; the ninth and tenth: the flags of missions 9-10 and a 5-bit checksum (XOR of the score's four bytes). Witness: L1 1 (menu).
> A non-zero score: m1complete frame 22160 ("C5989999PB", score 500, mission 1), m4complete 21956 ("H59K9999PK", score 2500, missions 1 and 4).

$CE7D b DecodedFlags 10 ; The ten completion flags DecodePassword makes
> Copied to MissionsDone by PasswordScreen.

$CE87 b DecodedScore 4 ; The score DecodePassword makes
> Copied to Score by PasswordScreen.

$CE8B c DecodePassword ; Unpack the ten values at $CEFB into DecodedFlags and DecodedScore; Z when the checksum agrees
$CE97 DecodeLoop
$CEA0 DecodeFlagA
$CEAD DecodeFlagB
$CECB DecodeFlag9
$CED6 DecodeFlag10
$CEF0 DecodeChecksum
> Witness: L1 1 (menu).

$CEF6 b PasswordText 16 ; Text: the password line; its ten characters at $CEFB
> Written by EncodePassword, PasswordToChars and CharsToPassword (the values), printed by ShowPassword.
$CEFB PasswordValues
@res texts/password-text.bin

$CF06 c ScoreBits ; A = the next A bits of the score at HL' (E' bits left in the byte)
$CF0B ScoreBitsLoop
$CF16 ScoreBitsNext
> Witness: L1 8 (menu).

$CF1C c PasswordToChars ; Map the ten values at $CEFB to characters through PasswordAlphabet
$CF25 ToCharsLoop
> Twelve `NOP`s at `$CF26` are room left for a patch. Witness: L1 1 (menu).

$CF3E c CharsToPassword ; Map the ten typed characters to their places in PasswordAlphabet
$CF46 ToValuesLoop
$CF4E AlphabetSearch
$CF54 AlphabetFound
> An unknown character gives 4 (B = 0 after the search: 36 AND 31); D, E, F and G give 0-3 like 9876. Witness: L1 1 (menu).

$CF5F b PasswordAlphabet 12 ; The 36 password characters: "9876543210ZYXWVUTSRQPONMLKJIHABCDEFG"
> Read by PasswordToChars and CharsToPassword.
@res texts/password-alphabet.bin

$CF83 b CatalogueHead 2 ; Two zero bytes, then the catalogue's first 32 bytes copied by CopyCatalogueHead
> `$CF85-$CFA4` is written by CopyCatalogueHead `$70C6`, compared by IsCatalogueLoaded and printed (the title) by MissionCatalogueScreen. The image holds leftover code bytes here; `$CFA5-$CFA8` are never read.
$CF85 CatalogueTitle
$CFA5 ; leftover

